# UI theming and contrast

## ColorScheme first

- Prefer `Theme.of(context).colorScheme` for text and fills on cards, sheets, and chrome (`onSurface`, `onSurfaceVariant`, `surfaceContainerHighest`, `primary`, `onPrimary`).
- Avoid a single hard-coded brand color as both badge fill **and** label ink on unknown surfaces — it can disappear on dark backgrounds. Use theme-aware pairs (`colorScheme.primary` / `onPrimary`, or surfaces + `onSurface`).
- Avoid bare `Colors.grey[500]` / `shade500` for body or label text on dark surfaces; use `colorScheme.onSurfaceVariant` or `onSurface` with opacity.

## Date / time pickers

RefereeIQ currently ships a single light `ThemeData` in [`lib/theme.dart`](lib/theme.dart). When you add `showDatePicker` / `showTimePicker`, wrap them in a `Theme` copy whose `colorScheme` keeps readable ink on selected segments (avoid using seed `primary` alone as dialog ink on unknown surfaces). Prefer `Theme.of(context).colorScheme` overrides over hard-coded greys.

## Dialogs and AppBar actions

- Trailing AppBar actions (`TextButton.icon`, etc.) should not hug the screen edge: use sufficient end padding (e.g. `symmetric(horizontal: 12)` or `EdgeInsets.only(right: 16)`) so actions align visually with leading icon spacing.

## Chips, badges, status pills

- Test light and dark theme: label contrast must meet readable text on the pill background.
- On dark grey live/event surfaces, use light label color or `colorScheme.onSurface` with a tinted border, not black-on-black.
